      <div class="bio">
        <p>
          I am a <strong>Postdoctoral Fellow at the Technical University of Denmark</strong>.
          I do research on formal models of reasoning and rationality in multi-agent and epistemic settings.
          My work has been primarily in logic, focused on modeling various forms of belief
          and belief formation under unawareness and inattention. I am also interested in implicit bias,
          where I am working to apply formal reasoning with the aim to get clear on some fundamental concepts in the study of implicit bias.
        </p>
        <p>
          I earned my PhD at the University of Copenhagen, 
          with a dissertation on logical models of reasoning 
          that mainly explores how this is affected by explicit and 
          implicit cognitive phenomena, such as attention, awareness, and implicit inferences.
        </p>
          <p>
          Beyond questions of rationality, I am fascinated by methodological issues such as: 
          "why are mathematical models so illuminating about reality, when they abstract away so many details?"
        </p>
      </div>
    </header>

    <section id="research">
      <h2>Research</h2>
      <div class="grid">
        <article class="card">
          <h3>Attention &amp; awareness</h3>
          <p>Logical models of how cognitive limitations affect learning
             and reasoning about other agents.</p>
        </article>

        <article class="card">
          <h3>Belief change</h3>
          <p>Dynamic epistemic approaches to belief contraction, including
             models that do not require plausibility orderings.</p>
        </article>
      </div>
